1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,310
Have you ever wondered where all those
microplastics actually come from?

2
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:09,690
When we hear the word microplastics, most
of us probably think of plastic bottles.

3
00:00:10,717 --> 00:00:12,727
Plastic bags. Food packaging.

4
00:00:12,857 --> 00:00:16,917
Synthetic clothes shedding tiny
fibres in the washing machine.

5
00:00:16,997 --> 00:00:18,507
Maybe cosmetics.

6
00:00:19,257 --> 00:00:23,427
Or simply plastic waste slowly breaking
down into smaller and smaller pieces.

7
00:00:24,383 --> 00:00:27,373
And all of these things really
do contribute to the problem.

8
00:00:28,263 --> 00:00:30,683
But there is another major
source of tiny plastic

9
00:00:30,763 --> 00:00:34,293
particles that most people
probably don't think about.

10
00:00:35,169 --> 00:00:38,299
And you probably have some of
it very close to you right now.

11
00:00:39,041 --> 00:00:40,691
The tyres on your car.

12
00:00:43,347 --> 00:00:45,597
You probably know that tyres wear out.

13
00:00:46,347 --> 00:00:47,937
The tread slowly disappears.

14
00:00:49,167 --> 00:00:51,527
After tens of thousands
of kilometres, the tyres

15
00:00:51,607 --> 00:00:53,997
are worn enough that you
have to replace them.

16
00:00:54,647 --> 00:00:56,087
But have you ever stopped to think about

17
00:00:56,167 --> 00:00:58,997
where all that missing
material actually goes?

18
00:00:59,647 --> 00:01:01,737
Because it doesn't simply disappear.

19
00:01:02,617 --> 00:01:05,827
Every time you drive, the
tyres rub against the road.

20
00:01:06,377 --> 00:01:08,967
Tiny amounts of material are worn away.

21
00:01:09,777 --> 00:01:11,407
Some of it stays on the road.

22
00:01:12,097 --> 00:01:15,167
Some gets blown around by
traffic and moving air.

23
00:01:15,937 --> 00:01:19,987
And when it rains, some of it is washed
away into drains and towards rivers.

24
00:01:20,885 --> 00:01:24,395
Some of these particles are so small
that you can't see them at all.

25
00:01:26,895 --> 00:01:29,785
And a modern tyre isn't
simply a piece of rubber.

26
00:01:30,617 --> 00:01:34,477
It is a complex mixture of natural
rubber, synthetic polymers,

27
00:01:34,557 --> 00:01:38,367
carbon-based materials and a
variety of chemical additives.

28
00:01:39,353 --> 00:01:43,943
As the tyre wears, it produces what
scientists call tyre wear particles.

29
00:01:45,153 --> 00:01:49,063
And a portion of these particles falls
into the category of microplastics.

30
00:01:49,969 --> 00:01:52,179
But that phrase deserves a footnote.

31
00:01:52,589 --> 00:01:54,419
A tyre is not simply plastic.

32
00:01:55,549 --> 00:01:57,829
The tread is a blend of
natural rubber, which grows

33
00:01:57,909 --> 00:02:00,779
on trees, and synthetic
rubbers, which do not.

34
00:02:01,525 --> 00:02:06,095
And by the time a particle has left the
road, it usually isn't pure tread at all.

35
00:02:07,145 --> 00:02:11,475
It is tyre rubber with road surface
ground into it, in roughly equal parts.

36
00:02:12,439 --> 00:02:16,509
That is why scientists prefer to
say tyre and road wear particles.

37
00:02:17,679 --> 00:02:21,969
Whether those count as microplastics
depends on the definition you use.

38
00:02:22,585 --> 00:02:25,015
They are not quite a
fragment of a plastic bottle,

39
00:02:25,325 --> 00:02:27,855
and that is what makes
them so hard to study.

40
00:02:30,305 --> 00:02:33,535
And there is another reason these
particles are hard to study.

41
00:02:34,325 --> 00:02:36,735
The problem may not be the
rubber particle itself.

42
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:40,330
A tyre contains hundreds
of different chemicals.

43
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:43,430
One of them is called 6PPD.

44
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:48,270
It is there to protect the rubber from
ozone, so that the tyre lasts longer.

45
00:02:48,999 --> 00:02:51,929
It does that by reacting with
ozone before the rubber can.

46
00:02:52,699 --> 00:02:58,249
And one of the things that reaction leaves
behind is a chemical called 6PPD-quinone.

47
00:02:58,910 --> 00:03:01,600
And that one is extremely
toxic to some fish.

48
00:03:02,430 --> 00:03:04,770
It has been linked to
mass deaths of coho salmon

49
00:03:04,850 --> 00:03:07,980
in streams near busy
roads, after heavy rain.

50
00:03:08,779 --> 00:03:10,759
So sometimes the question
isn't what happens

51
00:03:10,839 --> 00:03:13,429
when a piece of tyre
enters the environment.

52
00:03:14,539 --> 00:03:17,069
It is what that tyre
releases along the way.

53
00:03:19,719 --> 00:03:22,679
So every time a car
drives down a road, it is

54
00:03:22,759 --> 00:03:25,749
gradually leaving a tiny
part of its tyres behind.

55
00:03:26,641 --> 00:03:30,661
Individually, the amount produced by
one car doesn't sound very impressive.

56
00:03:30,741 --> 00:03:34,011
But add it up over the life of a tyre.

57
00:03:34,825 --> 00:03:36,885
A car tyre is thought
to lose something like a

58
00:03:36,965 --> 00:03:39,935
tenth to a fifth of its own
weight while it is used.

59
00:03:40,945 --> 00:03:43,315
For a typical tyre that
is about a kilogram.

60
00:03:44,011 --> 00:03:46,121
Several kilograms for a full set.

61
00:03:46,991 --> 00:03:50,281
Scattered, invisibly, along
every road you have ever driven.

62
00:03:51,106 --> 00:03:53,776
And there are well over a
billion vehicles on this planet.

63
00:03:54,806 --> 00:03:56,416
Each one has several tyres.

64
00:03:56,786 --> 00:03:59,456
And all of them are
constantly wearing down.

65
00:04:00,273 --> 00:04:02,573
That's why tyre wear is considered one of

66
00:04:02,653 --> 00:04:05,243
the major sources of
microplastic pollution.

67
00:04:06,235 --> 00:04:09,875
The exact numbers vary considerably
between studies, because

68
00:04:09,955 --> 00:04:13,605
researchers use different definitions,
measurements and assumptions.

69
00:04:14,651 --> 00:04:17,161
But the basic idea is not controversial.

70
00:04:17,991 --> 00:04:21,701
A lot of material is leaving our
tyres and entering the environment.

71
00:04:24,401 --> 00:04:26,051
So where does it all end up?

72
00:04:26,741 --> 00:04:30,371
The models disagree on the details,
but they agree on the shape of it.

73
00:04:31,091 --> 00:04:33,561
Most of the material
stays close to the road,

74
00:04:33,751 --> 00:04:36,521
trapped in the asphalt
or in the soil beside it.

75
00:04:37,211 --> 00:04:39,721
A large part is carried away by rain.

76
00:04:40,576 --> 00:04:43,266
And something like a tenth
simply goes into the air.

77
00:04:43,676 --> 00:04:46,986
Which means some of it is not
travelling to a river at all.

78
00:04:47,696 --> 00:04:49,386
It is travelling to your lungs.

79
00:04:52,086 --> 00:04:55,176
And tyres aren't the only
source of particles from cars.

80
00:04:56,006 --> 00:04:57,356
There are also brakes.

81
00:04:58,286 --> 00:05:02,636
Every time you slow down, the brake
pads and other components wear down too.

82
00:05:03,365 --> 00:05:07,995
This produces brake dust, a mixture
containing metals and other materials.

83
00:05:09,245 --> 00:05:12,025
It isn't simply microplastic,
but it is another

84
00:05:12,105 --> 00:05:15,375
significant source of tiny
particles from road traffic.

85
00:05:16,241 --> 00:05:17,501
And there is also the road itself.

86
00:05:17,581 --> 00:05:20,011
Tyres wear. Brakes wear.

87
00:05:20,101 --> 00:05:21,531
Road surfaces wear.

88
00:05:22,311 --> 00:05:26,041
And traffic can stir up particles
that have already settled on the road.

89
00:05:26,711 --> 00:05:29,141
So when we talk about emissions from cars,

90
00:05:29,491 --> 00:05:31,941
the exhaust pipe is
only part of the story.

91
00:05:32,691 --> 00:05:35,181
These are sometimes called
non-exhaust emissions.

92
00:05:37,901 --> 00:05:41,831
And this brings us to something that
may sound slightly ridiculous at first.

93
00:05:42,901 --> 00:05:44,521
You may have heard
about the European Union

94
00:05:44,601 --> 00:05:47,451
introducing new requirements
for vehicle emissions.

95
00:05:48,410 --> 00:05:51,560
But Euro seven isn't
only about exhaust gases.

96
00:05:52,110 --> 00:05:54,630
For the first time,
Europe puts a number on

97
00:05:54,710 --> 00:05:57,120
how much dust your brakes
are allowed to shed.

98
00:05:58,113 --> 00:05:59,793
Three milligrams per
kilometre for a battery

99
00:05:59,873 --> 00:06:02,983
electric car, seven for everything else.

100
00:06:03,793 --> 00:06:06,723
Tyres are in there too,
but in a much weaker form.

101
00:06:07,093 --> 00:06:09,963
Those limits have not
actually been written yet.

102
00:06:10,761 --> 00:06:14,081
The regulation prints the
headings, leaves every single limit

103
00:06:14,161 --> 00:06:17,791
blank, and hands the job to a
United Nations working group.

104
00:06:20,311 --> 00:06:22,601
And here is the part
that people get wrong.

105
00:06:23,331 --> 00:06:24,721
The rules set a limit.

106
00:06:25,231 --> 00:06:26,961
They do not say how to meet it.

107
00:06:27,685 --> 00:06:30,975
And one of the routes they are
trying sounds almost like a joke.

108
00:06:31,905 --> 00:06:33,395
A vacuum cleaner for your brakes.

109
00:06:34,525 --> 00:06:37,305
Not a household vacuum
cleaner strapped to the wheel.

110
00:06:37,385 --> 00:06:40,285
But one French system
cuts a groove in the brake

111
00:06:40,365 --> 00:06:43,435
pad and sucks the dust
out through the caliper.

112
00:06:44,159 --> 00:06:46,629
On test rigs it captures most of it.

113
00:06:47,439 --> 00:06:49,409
Nobody is required to fit one.

114
00:06:50,019 --> 00:06:52,929
It is simply one answer to
a limit that has to be met.

115
00:06:53,731 --> 00:06:56,841
And suddenly, the idea
doesn't sound quite so crazy.

116
00:06:57,711 --> 00:07:01,321
Because if you know that a brake is
constantly producing tiny particles...

117
00:07:01,711 --> 00:07:05,081
why not try to catch them
before they spread everywhere?

118
00:07:07,721 --> 00:07:09,871
And here's another interesting part.

119
00:07:10,601 --> 00:07:13,231
Electric cars don't make
this problem disappear.

120
00:07:14,155 --> 00:07:17,895
They don't have exhaust emissions
from an internal combustion engine.

121
00:07:17,975 --> 00:07:21,205
And most of them barely
touch their brake pads.

122
00:07:22,061 --> 00:07:24,161
They slow down with the motor, recovering

123
00:07:24,241 --> 00:07:27,371
energy instead of rubbing
a pad against a disc.

124
00:07:28,241 --> 00:07:32,091
Measurements suggest this can cut
brake particles by well over half.

125
00:07:32,889 --> 00:07:36,179
Europe already asks them for
three milligrams instead of seven.

126
00:07:37,209 --> 00:07:41,159
But no motor can help with the
tyres. They still have tyres.

127
00:07:41,309 --> 00:07:42,819
They still drive on roads.

128
00:07:43,707 --> 00:07:46,807
In fact, because some electric
vehicles are heavier than comparable

129
00:07:46,887 --> 00:07:51,997
combustion-engine cars, tyre wear can
be an interesting issue of its own.

130
00:07:52,678 --> 00:07:57,688
So even a car with zero tailpipe emissions
can still produce particles as it moves.

131
00:08:00,368 --> 00:08:02,398
So what can we actually do about it?

132
00:08:03,088 --> 00:08:05,268
There are obvious
technological approaches.

133
00:08:05,348 --> 00:08:09,738
Tyres could be designed to last
longer and produce less wear.

134
00:08:10,468 --> 00:08:12,218
Their materials could be improved.

135
00:08:13,185 --> 00:08:15,125
Road runoff could potentially be filtered

136
00:08:15,205 --> 00:08:17,935
before it reaches rivers
and other waterways.

137
00:08:18,955 --> 00:08:21,365
But there is also a much simpler solution.

138
00:08:22,115 --> 00:08:24,285
Don't wear the tyre
out in the first place.

139
00:08:25,133 --> 00:08:26,683
Driving style matters.

140
00:08:27,633 --> 00:08:29,933
Hard acceleration,
aggressive cornering and

141
00:08:30,013 --> 00:08:32,783
heavy braking all put
additional stress on tyres.

142
00:08:33,743 --> 00:08:37,013
But even the gentlest driver
cannot eliminate tyre wear.

143
00:08:37,843 --> 00:08:42,153
Because that's what a tyre is
designed to do. It grips the road.

144
00:08:42,523 --> 00:08:46,073
And some of its material is
inevitably lost in the process.

145
00:08:48,843 --> 00:08:52,153
And this brings us to the most
uncomfortable part of the story.

146
00:08:52,943 --> 00:08:55,033
What does all of this actually do to us?

147
00:08:55,789 --> 00:08:58,379
The honest answer is that
we don't fully know yet.

148
00:08:59,369 --> 00:09:03,819
Microplastics have been detected in
water, food, air and human tissues.

149
00:09:04,783 --> 00:09:09,273
And nanoplastics are an even newer
and more difficult subject to study.

150
00:09:09,999 --> 00:09:12,759
But finding particles in
the human body doesn't

151
00:09:12,839 --> 00:09:15,329
automatically mean that
they are causing disease.

152
00:09:16,282 --> 00:09:18,042
We still don't know
what levels of exposure

153
00:09:18,122 --> 00:09:21,072
are harmful, or what happens over decades.

154
00:09:22,138 --> 00:09:24,038
And this is one reason why simply calling

155
00:09:24,118 --> 00:09:26,768
all of this microplastics
can be misleading.

156
00:09:27,818 --> 00:09:30,108
The research is still
catching up with the problem.

157
00:09:32,878 --> 00:09:35,748
And perhaps that's the strangest
thing about all of this.

158
00:09:36,578 --> 00:09:39,108
We've been driving cars
for more than a century.

159
00:09:39,758 --> 00:09:42,068
We've always known that tyres wear out.

160
00:09:42,813 --> 00:09:45,083
We can literally see
the tread disappearing.

161
00:09:46,023 --> 00:09:50,713
But only relatively recently have we
started asking a much more basic question.

162
00:09:51,723 --> 00:09:53,793
Where did all that missing material go?

163
00:09:54,527 --> 00:09:59,037
It didn't disappear. It was gradually
scattered into the world around us.

164
00:09:59,830 --> 00:10:03,920
And some of it became particles so
small that we can no longer see them.

165
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:08,770
So the next time you look
at a worn-out tyre...

166
00:10:09,320 --> 00:10:12,450
remember that it isn't just a
tyre that has become thinner.

167
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:16,330
A small part of that tyre has
become part of the environment.

168
00:10:17,515 --> 00:10:19,385
Which leaves one open question.

169
00:10:20,295 --> 00:10:23,145
Can we learn to catch those
particles before they get away...

170
00:10:23,815 --> 00:10:25,905
or does the tyre itself have to change?
